<pdd-reason>The chyron across the top: a live TV ticker of what the agents are doing right now. It is the single element that turns a voting board into a broadcast — proof that something is happening even when the board itself is still.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"Broadcast","signature":"function Broadcast(props: BroadcastProps): JSX.Element","returns":"the chyron strip, cycling one message at a time"},
  {"name":"BroadcastProps","signature":"type BroadcastProps = { messages?: BroadcastMessage[]; onSuccessPhase?: () => void }","returns":"the component contract"},
  {"name":"BroadcastMessage","signature":"type BroadcastMessage = { icon: string; agent: string; text: string; success?: boolean }","returns":"one ticker line"},
  {"name":"DEMO_BROADCAST","signature":"const DEMO_BROADCAST: BroadcastMessage[]","returns":"the scripted agent workflow shown until US-11 wires Realtime"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

Implement `frontend/src/components/broadcast.tsx`.

<responsibility>
Cycle one agent status line at a time across the top of the forum, and tell the shell when a build has landed.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT subscribe to Realtime or call `api_client` — it renders the messages it is handed. US-11 replaces the default array with a live feed by passing `messages`, and no markup changes when it does.
This module DOES NOT render pitch content. The public ticker carries phase and micro-copy only; an unscreened pitch's text reaching this strip would be exactly the leak screening exists to prevent.
This module DOES NOT refresh the sandbox; it reports the success phase and the shell decides.
This module DOES NOT import `styles.css`; the shell imports it once for the whole app.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
**Chyron** — the broadcast term for the lower-third ticker. Here it sits at the top, above the masthead, and it is the first thing that moves on the page.
**Success phase** — a scripted line marked `success`, meaning a build reached the sandbox. It is what lights up the refresh control in the preview panel.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY hold the current message index and an interval timer.
- MUST NOT call `fetch`, `api_client`, or Supabase.
- MUST NOT add a hex value, font, radius, or animation — compose only `broadcast`, `broadcast-live`, `broadcast-label`, `broadcast-msg`, `is-success`, `live-dot`.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Render `broadcast` containing, in order, a `broadcast-live` group with the blinking `live-dot` and the word `LIVE`, the `broadcast-label` reading `AI CREATOR BROADCAST`, and the current `broadcast-msg`.
R2 (MUST): Render each message as its icon, the agent name in `<strong>`, then the text.
R3 (MUST): Advance to the next message every 5 seconds, wrapping at the end, and clear the interval on unmount. A ticker that keeps firing after the component is gone is a leak that only shows up in tests as a stray act warning.
R4 (MUST): Re-key the message element on each change so the slide-in animation replays. Without a changing key React updates the text in place and the strip goes visually dead.
R5 (MUST): Add `is-success` to a message marked `success` and call `onSuccessPhase` when one becomes current. That is the hand-off that makes the preview panel's refresh button pulse.
R6 (MUST): Default `messages` to `DEMO_BROADCAST` so the chyron is never empty. The strip is part of the frozen layout, and an empty one collapses the page's top edge.
R7 (MUST): Give the strip `role="status"` with `aria-live="polite"`, so the ticker is announced without interrupting.
R8 (MUST NOT): Render more than one message at a time, or render the strip twice. It is one line of the frozen skeleton.
R9 (MUST): Ship `DEMO_BROADCAST` covering the pipeline in order — Guardagent screening, the community voting, the PM Agent synthesising duplicates, the Architect Agent testing constraints, the Janitor Agent sweeping the Vault, and a final Ship Agent line marked `success`. It is the demo's narration of the architecture, so the agents and their order match the real pipeline rather than being decorative.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
The scripted array is a placeholder with a real seam, not a mock mode: US-11 will pass live
`broadcast_events` rows through the `messages` prop and delete nothing. That is why the shape is
`{icon, agent, text, success}` rather than the Postgres row — the ticker's vocabulary should not
become the database's.

Five seconds per line is a demo-tuned number: long enough to read, short enough that a judge sees
three different agents inside fifteen seconds of watching.
</pdd>

